# Example keyboard_joystick mapping file (keyboard_joystick -c <file>).
#
# One rule per line:
#   <joystick> <key> <target>
#
#   <joystick>  joystick number, starting at 1. The highest number used
#               decides how many virtual joysticks are created.
#   <key>       key name, case-insensitive, optional KEY_ prefix
#               (W, w, KEY_W, LEFTSHIFT, BACKSLASH, KP1, F1, ...).
#   <target>    UP, DOWN, LEFT or RIGHT for an axis direction, or a
#               button name, BTN_ prefix optional: A, B, C, X, Y, Z,
#               TL, TR, TL2, TR2, SELECT, START, MODE, THUMBL, THUMBR.
#
# Blank lines and '#' comments are ignored. Several keys may drive the
# same target, and one key may drive several targets. A file replaces
# the built-in mapping entirely, so list every rule you want.
#
# This example reproduces the built-in default mapping; dump the
# effective one at any time with "keyboard_joystick --print-map".

# joystick 1: WASD to move, T/F/G/H diamond for the buttons
1 W            UP
1 S            DOWN
1 A            LEFT
1 D            RIGHT
1 G            A
1 H            B
1 T            X
1 F            Y

# joystick 2: arrow keys to move, Ins/Home/PgUp/ScLk/End cluster for the
# buttons (a diamond around Home, which is the extra START button)
2 UP           UP
2 DOWN         DOWN
2 LEFT         LEFT
2 RIGHT        RIGHT
2 END          A
2 PAGEUP       B
2 SCROLLLOCK   X
2 INSERT       Y
2 HOME         START
